<Project>

  <!--
    Solution-wide build settings. Every project inherits these; MSBuild imports the nearest
    Directory.Build.props walking up from each .csproj.

    Note what that means for the submodule: projects under knowledge/ find the Directory.Build.props
    at the root of THAT repository first and stop there, so this file governs only this repository's
    projects. The two sets of settings cannot interfere with each other.

    ==========================================================================================
    EnforceCodeStyleInBuild
    ==========================================================================================
    The C# compiler no longer emits CS1998 ("This async method lacks 'await' operators and will
    run synchronously"). It was not downgraded or suppressed - it was deleted from the compiler
    in dotnet/roslyn#80144, after dotnet/roslyn#77001 concluded the warning was doing more harm
    than good: people silenced it with meaningless `await Task.FromResult(...)` calls, which cost
    a state machine and hide nothing.

    Roslyn shipped a replacement in dotnet/roslyn#81835 - IDE0390 and IDE0391 - but as opt-in IDE
    code-style analyzers rather than compiler warnings. Two things are needed to make an IDE rule
    fail a command-line or CI build: this property, and an explicit severity in .editorconfig.
    Neither alone is enough. With the property set but no severity entry, IDE0390 produces nothing
    at build time. With the severity but no property, it is editor-only. Removing either silently
    disables the guard, which is why both carry a comment pointing at the other.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ==========================================================================================
    GenerateDocumentationFile - so that CS1570 (malformed XML doc) fails the build
    ==========================================================================================
    The compiler only parses /// comments as XML when it is asked to emit a documentation file.
    Without this, a doc comment can carry unbalanced tags indefinitely: the build stays green,
    -warnaserror stays green, and nothing notices until someone reads the file. In a repository
    whose doc comments are a large part of what it is for, that matters more than usual.

    The point is CS1570 specifically. The completeness diagnostics are suppressed, because the
    rule here is that whatever comments exist must parse - not that every public member must carry
    one:

      CS1591  missing XML comment for a publicly visible member
      CS1573  some parameters documented, others not
      CS1574  cref pointing at something that will not resolve

    With those suppressed, CI's -warnaserror turns any future malformed doc comment into a failed
    build without also demanding a doc-completeness pass.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573;CS1574</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
